n- Third Party Designee\n- Sign Your Return\n- - Court-Appointed Conservator, Guardian, or Other Fiduciary\n- Child’s Return\n- Requirements for a Paper Return\n- Requirements for an Electronic Return\n- - Self-Select PIN.\n- Practitioner PIN.\n- Form 8453.\n- Identity Protection PIN\n- - Phone Number and Email Address\n- Paid Preparer Must Sign Your Return\n- Assemble Your Return\n- 1040 - Main Contents\n- - Tax Table\n- General Information\n- - Introduction\n- How To Avoid Common Mistakes\n- Innocent Spouse Relief\n- Income Tax Withholding and Estimated Tax Payments for 2026\n- Secure Your Tax Records From Identity Theft\n- - Protect yourself from suspicious emails, texts, and social media messages, phishing schemes, and phone scams.\n- How Do You Make a Gift To Reduce Debt Held By the Public?\n- How Long Should Records Be Kept?\n- Amended Return\n- Need a Copy of Your Tax Return Information?\n- Past Due Returns\n- How To Get Tax Help\n- - Tax reform.\n- Preparing and filing your tax return.\n- Free options for tax preparation.\n- Using online tools to help prepare your return.\n- Need someone to prepare your tax return?\n- Employers can register to use Business Services Online.\n- Business tax account.\n- IRS social media.\n- Over-the-Phone Interpreter (OPI) Service.\n- Accessibility Helpline available for taxpayers with disabilities.\n- Alternative media preference.\n- Disasters.\n- Getting tax forms and publications.\n- Mobile-friendly forms.\n- Getting tax publications and instructions in eBook format.\n- Access your online account (individual taxpayers only).\n- Get a transcript of your return.\n- Tax Pro Account.\n- Using direct deposit.\n- Reporting and resolving your tax-related identity theft issues.\n- Ways to check on the status of your refund.\n- Making a tax payment.\n- What if I can’t pay now?\n\n...\n\n- Checking the status of your amended return.\n\n...\n\n- Interest and Penalties\n\n---\n\nTitle: Instructions for Form 1040-NR (2025) | Internal Revenue Service\nURL: https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040nr\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nException 1.\n- Exception 2.\n- Exception 3.\n- Effectively Connected Pension Distributions\n- Fully Taxable Pensions and Annuities\n- Exception.\n- Partially Taxable Pensions and Annuities\n...\n- Lump-Sum Distributions\n- Line 5c\n- Line 6—Reserved for Future\n\n---\n\nTitle: Instructions for Form 8814 (2025) | Internal Revenue Service\nURL: https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8814\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n#### Capital Gain Distributions\n\nLine 3.\n\nEnter the capital gain distributions your child received in 2025. Capital gain distributions should be shown in box 2a of Form 1099-DIV.\n\nThe amount of these distributions that is added to your income must be reported on Schedule D (Form 1040), Capital Gains and Losses, line 13, or, if you aren’t required to file Schedule D, on Form 1040, 1040-SR, or 1040-NR, line 7a, whichever applies.\n\nThis is the amount on Form 8814, line 10. (The amount on Form 8814, line 10, may be less than the amount on Form 8814, line 3, because lines 7 through 12 of the form divide the $2,700 base amount on Form 8814, line 5, between the child’s qualified dividends, capital gain distributions, and other interest and dividend income, reducing each of those amounts.)\n\n...\n\nLine 9.\n\nInclude this amount on Form 1040, 1040-SR, or 1040-NR, lines 3a and 3b. Enter “Form 8814” and this amount on the dotted line next to those lines unless you file Schedule B (Form 1040).\n\nIf you file Schedule B, include this amount on line 5 and identify it as from “Form 8814.” Complete Schedule B as instructed. Also include this amount on Form 1040, 1040-SR, or 1040-NR, line 3a.\n\nYou must file Schedule B if this amount plus the parents’ dividends is more than $1,500.\n\n---\n\nTitle: IRS Form 1040-SR | Fill Out Printable PDF Forms Online\nURL: https://formspal.com/pdf-forms/irs-1040-sr/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nCapital gain, total income and adjusted gross income\n\nLine 7a: Capital gain or (loss). Attach Schedule D if required. Line 7b: Check if: Schedule D not required; Includes child's capital gain or (loss).\n\nLine 8: Additional income from Schedule 1, line 10. Line 9: Add lines 1z, 2b, 3b, 4b, 5b, 6b, 7a, and 8. This is your total income.\n\nLine 10: Adjustments to income from Schedule 1, line 26. Line 11a: Subtract line 10 from line 9. This is your adjusted gross income. Line 11b: Amount from line 11a (adjusted gross income).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Form 1040 Line 7: Capital Gain or Loss, 7a and 7b (2025)\nURL: https://clearmoneyguide.com/form-1040-line-7-capital-gain-or-loss/\nPublished: 2026-10-17T22:25:13.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nUpdated October 17, 2026. Quick answer: Line 7a is your capital gain or loss. If you sold a capital asset, the instructions say to complete Form 8949 and Schedule D, and the result goes on the line. A narrow exception lets some filers enter only capital gain distributions from Form 1099-DIV and check the Schedule D not required box on 7b.\n\n...\n\n## What goes on line 7a\n\nThe form caption is “Capital gain or (loss). Attach Schedule D if required”. The instructions open with the general rule: “If you sold a capital asset, such as a stock, bond, or digital asset, you must complete and attach Form 8949 and Schedule D.” The schedule is explained on the Schedule D page, and the broker form behind many sales is on the 1099-B proceeds and cost basis page. The rest of the return is mapped on Form 1040 line by line.\n\n...\n\nThe two conditions are “You have no capital losses, and your only capital gains are capital gain distributions from Form(s) 1099-DIV, box 2a (or substitute statements); and” “None of the Form(s) 1099-DIV (or substitute statements) have an amount in box 2b (unrecaptured section\n\n...\n\nWhen Exception 1 applies, “enter your total capital gain distributions (from box 2a of Form(s) 1099-DIV) on line 7a and check the box” for Schedule D not required on line 7b. For nominee amounts the instructions say “If you received capital gain distributions as a nominee (that is, they were paid to you but actually belong to someone else), report on line 7a only the amount that belongs to you.”\n\n...\n\n## Line 7b: the two boxes\n\nThe form caption is “Schedule D not required Includes child’s capital gain or (loss)”. The instructions say “If Exception 1 applies, check the” Schedule D not required box on line 7b.\n\nThe second box is for a child’s amounts: “If you are including your child’s capital gain or (loss) in the total on line 7a, check the” child’s capital gain or loss box on line 7b “and enter the amount from Form 8814, line 10, in the entry space.” “For more information, see the Instructions for Form 8814.”\n\n## Where the number comes from\n\nLine 7a comes from Schedule D when Schedule D is filed, and from Form 1099-DIV, box 2a, when Exception 1 applies. Sales of stock and other assets are reported to you on Form 1099-B; the short-term and long-term split is shown on the 1099-B box 2 page. A calculator for the resulting tax is at the capital gains tax calculator.\n\n## Where it goes next\n\nLine 7a is part of the income total. The form’s line 9 reads “Add lines 1z, 2b, 3b, 4b, 5b, 6b, 7a, and 8. This is your total income”. Line 7a is also an input for the social security worksheet described on the lines 6a and 6b page. The total is on the lines 9, 10 and 11 page.\n\n...\n\nThis page reads the 2025 Form 1040 and its 2025 instructions. Those are the files irs.gov was serving as the current ones on 2026-10-17, and they are for the tax year 2025 return, filed in 2026. Check the tax year printed on the form in front of you and use the line numbers on that year’s form. For the 2025 edition, the instructions state: “The lines on Forms 1040 and 1040-SR are the same.” Line 7 is split into 7a and 7b in this edition; the exceptions and the line 7b boxes are as quoted above."
